{%- liquid
  assign wrapper_class = 'my-story'
  if section.settings.quote != blank
    assign wrapper_class = wrapper_class | append: ' my-story--quoted'
  endif

  assign has_text = false
  if section.settings.heading != blank
    assign has_text = true
  endif
  if section.settings.body != blank
    assign has_text = true
  endif
  if section.settings.signature != blank
    assign has_text = true
  endif

  comment
    The phone `sizes` factor is the share of the content column the photograph
    actually occupies once it has been pushed in from the left, so it tracks
    the offset setting rather than assuming its default.
  endcomment
  assign mobile_width_factor = 100 | minus: section.settings.image_offset | divided_by: 100.0

  comment
    How many lines the merchant broke the quote into. On desktop the quote is
    out of the flow, so the layout has to be told how much room to leave for
    it — deriving that from the real line count means a two-line or a
    four-line quote both clear the story underneath instead of only the
    three-line one the design happens to use.
  endcomment
  assign quote_lines = section.settings.quote | newline_to_br | split: '<br />' | size

  comment
    The tilt is handed over a second time without its sign, because the lane
    below needs its magnitude to work out how much taller the rotation makes
    the quote — and CSS has no absolute value.
  endcomment
  assign quote_tilt = section.settings.quote_rotation | abs
-%}

{%- capture image_sizes -%}
  (min-width: 1598px) 526px, (min-width: 750px) calc((100vw - 158px) * 0.41), calc((100vw - 60px) * {{ mobile_width_factor }})
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ wrapper_class }}"
  style="
    --quote-size: {{ section.settings.quote_size }}px;
    --quote-lines: {{ quote_lines }};
    --quote-tilt: {{ quote_tilt }}deg;
    --quote-rotation: {{ section.settings.quote_rotation }}deg;
    --image-offset: {{ section.settings.image_offset }}%;
    --image-position: {{ section.settings.image_position }}%;
  "
>
  {%- if section.settings.quote != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The line breaks are the merchant's: the quote is set in a script face at
      a size where automatic wrapping lands badly, and the design breaks it
      into three deliberate lines. `escape` first so the setting can't inject
      markup, then `newline_to_br` turns the typed breaks into <br>.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <blockquote class="my-story__quote">
      {{- section.settings.quote | escape | newline_to_br -}}
    </blockquote>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The photograph is ~41% of the content column on desktop (two equal columns
    either side of an 18% channel) and the column stops growing at
    1440 - 158 = 1282px once the viewport passes 1598px, so 1282 * 0.41 = 526px
    is as large as it ever renders. Keep these in step with the layout settings
    in `config/settings_schema.json`.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <figure class="my-story__media">
    {%- if section.settings.image != blank -%}
      {{
        section.settings.image
        | image_url: width: 1600
        | image_tag:
            widths: '300,400,500,600,750,900,1100,1300,1600',
            sizes: image_sizes,
            loading: 'lazy',
            alt: section.settings.image.alt
      }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'image' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'my-story__placeholder' }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </figure>

  {%- if has_text -%}
    <div class="my-story__text">
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="my-story__heading">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if section.settings.body != blank -%}
        <div class="my-story__body">{{ section.settings.body }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if section.settings.signature != blank -%}
        <p class="my-story__signature">{{ section.settings.signature }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{% stylesheet %}
  /**
   * Unlike the other sections this one emits a single wrapper rather than a
   * run of siblings, because the quote is positioned against it. The wrapper
   * still lands in the content column on its own — `.shopify-section > *`
   * in critical.css sees to that.
   */
  .my-story {
    /**
     * The size the quote actually renders at, kept on the wrapper rather than
     * on the quote because the room reserved for the quote has to be worked
     * out from it too. The dial is a desktop measurement; a phone caps it
     * against the viewport so a large setting cannot run off the side.
     */
    --quote-rendered-size: min(calc(var(--quote-size, 60px) * 0.55), 7vw);

    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-section);
    padding-block: var(--spacing-section);
  }

  /**
   * A tilted line of text is taller than a level one by its own width times
   * the sine of the angle, and here the tilt pivots on the quote's left edge,
   * so the whole of that rise lands above the quote's flow position. Padding
   * it back on keeps a steep tilt inside the section instead of over whatever
   * sits above it. `10em` is the quote's measure — the design's longest line
   * is a little over ten of its em squares.
   *
   * The desktop rule pivots on the middle instead, so it corrects by half.
   */
  .my-story--quoted {
    padding-block-start: calc(
      var(--spacing-section) + var(--quote-rendered-size) * 10 * sin(var(--quote-tilt, 4deg))
    );
  }

  /**
   * The quote is the one part of the design that ignores the grid: script
   * face, tilted, and allowed to sit on top of the photograph.
   *
   * On a phone it stays in the flow and the photograph is pulled up beneath
   * it by the negative bottom margin — `--quote-overlap` is in `em`, so how
   * far the two pieces interlock scales with the quote rather than drifting
   * as the setting changes. `z-index` is what keeps it on top.
   *
   * `rotate` rather than `transform` so the tilt composes with the centring
   * translate the desktop rule adds instead of overwriting it.
   *
   * `align-self` rather than `width: max-content` is what shrink-wraps it
   * here: a max-content width would be handed to the wrapper as its own
   * min-content contribution — percentage `max-width` is ignored during
   * intrinsic sizing — and push the whole section wider than the phone.
   */
  .my-story__quote {
    --quote-overlap: 0.75em;

    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    align-self: start;
    margin-block-end: calc(-1 * (var(--spacing-section) + var(--quote-overlap)));
    rotate: var(--quote-rotation, -4deg);
    transform-origin: 0 50%;
    font-family: var(--font-script--family);
    font-size: var(--quote-rendered-size);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  /**
   * The photograph is pushed in from the left on a phone and runs out to the
   * right page margin. That asymmetry is what gives the quote a corner to sit
   * in — without it the two would simply stack.
   */
  .my-story__media {
    margin-inline-start: var(--image-offset, 20%);
    aspect-ratio: 9 / 10;
  }

  /**
   * `cover` already centres the crop; the setting exists because a photograph
   * whose subject sits high or low in the frame needs to be pulled that way by
   * hand. The percentage is the vertical focal point: 0% holds the top edge of
   * the photograph against the top of the frame, 100% the bottom edge against
   * the bottom. Horizontal stays centred — the frame is nearly square, so
   * there is little to choose along that axis.
   */
  .my-story__media img,
  .my-story__media svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center var(--image-position, 50%);
  }

  .my-story__text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  /**
   * The reset zeroes every margin, so the blank lines between the story's
   * paragraphs have to be put back. One `em` reads as a blank line at the
   * body step without opening the gaps the theme's section rhythm uses.
   */
  .my-story__body :where(p) + :where(p) {
    margin-block-start: 1em;
  }

  /* Signs off hard right, under the last line of the story. */
  .my-story__signature {
    align-self: end;
    font-family: var(--font-script--family);
    font-size: 2.75rem;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  @media (width >= 750px) {
    /**
     * Two equal columns either side of a wide channel. The channel is not
     * empty space for its own sake — it is where the quote sits, and the 18%
     * is measured off the design.
     *
     * Two rows, not one. The first is the quote's lane: empty in the left
     * column, photograph in the right. That is what puts clear air above the
     * heading for the quote to sit in, and it holds however long the story
     * runs — bottom-aligning the story against a fixed-ratio photograph would
     * only look right for copy of roughly the length the design was drawn
     * with, and would slide the heading up under the quote for anything
     * longer.
     *
     * The story lane has a floor so a short story still leaves the
     * photograph a sensible shape.
     */
    .my-story {
      --quote-rendered-size: var(--quote-size, 60px);

      /* Overridden below when there is a quote to leave room for. */
      --quote-lane: 0px;

      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      grid-template-rows: var(--quote-lane) minmax(18rem, auto);
      column-gap: 18%;
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    /**
     * The lane is the quote's own height, less the part of it that rides
     * above the photograph, plus the height the tilt adds, plus a little air.
     *
     * A tilted line of text is taller than a level one by its own width times
     * the sine of the angle, so the third term needs a width to work from.
     * `10em` is the quote's own measure — the design's longest line is a
     * little over ten of its em squares, and any line much longer than that
     * would be wrapping anyway. Every term is a multiple of the size setting,
     * so the lane tracks both dials across their whole range.
     */
    .my-story--quoted {
      --quote-lane: calc(
        var(--quote-rendered-size) * 1.15 * var(--quote-lines, 3) - var(--quote-rendered-size) * 0.7 +
          var(--quote-rendered-size) * 10 * sin(var(--quote-tilt, 4deg)) + 1.5rem
      );

      /* Room for the quote's first line to rise above the photograph. */
      padding-block-start: calc(var(--spacing-section) + var(--quote-rendered-size) * 0.7);
    }

    /* Explicit rows: the photograph precedes the story in the DOM, so sparse
       auto-placement would otherwise push the story down a row of its own. */
    .my-story__text {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
      align-self: end;
    }

    /* Down both rows, so its foot lands on the story's last line. */
    .my-story__media {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
      margin-inline-start: 0;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }

    /**
     * Out of the flow and centred on the section, so it spans the channel and
     * spills over the photograph's upper-left corner. Absolute positioning
     * resolves against the padding box, so the top padding added above for the
     * quote's own sake does not push the quote down with it.
     */
    .my-story__quote {
      position: absolute;

      /* Same half-tilt correction the phone gets from its top padding. */
      inset-block-start: calc(
        var(--spacing-section) + var(--quote-rendered-size) * 5 * sin(var(--quote-tilt, 4deg))
      );
      inset-inline-start: 50%;
      width: max-content;
      max-width: 100%;
      margin-block-end: 0;
      translate: -50% 0;
      transform-origin: 50% 50%;
      font-size: var(--quote-size, 60px);
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:general.my_story",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "t:labels.heading",
      "default": "My Story"
    },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "body",
      "label": "t:labels.paragraph",
      "default": "<p>It started on September 25, 2022, when I went on my first hike.<br/>Without any previous experience, I was wearing old running shoes.</p><p>There were 245 km ahead. I went from Porto to the Cathedral of Santiago, right now I’m getting a pleasant shiver as I write this.</p><p>I could not wish for a more beautiful road, no, I could not even imagine what it would bring me, where it would take me.<br/>Please, go hike yourself.</p><p>Your courage is the beginning of a new path.<br/>With love, respect and gratitude for yourself, your body.</p><p>And get comfortable shoes!</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "signature",
      "label": "t:labels.signature",
      "default": "Gerda"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "quote",
      "label": "t:labels.quote",
      "info": "t:info.quote",
      "default": "The journey of a\nthousand miles begins\nwith a single step."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "quote_size",
      "label": "t:labels.quote_size",
      "min": 32,
      "max": 96,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 60
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "quote_rotation",
      "label": "t:labels.quote_rotation",
      "info": "t:info.quote_rotation",
      "min": -12,
      "max": 0,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "deg",
      "default": -4
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "image",
      "label": "t:labels.image"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "image_position",
      "label": "t:labels.image_position",
      "info": "t:info.image_position",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 5,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 50
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "image_offset",
      "label": "t:labels.image_offset",
      "info": "t:info.image_offset",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 30,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "%",
      "default": 20
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "t:general.my_story"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
